\section{Ephemeral Cipher-State Evolution}
\label{sec:cipher-evolution}

The cryptographic enclosure operates under a continuously evolving internal
cipher-state, driven by the femtosecond nonce counter and the authenticated
256-bit verification key. Because the RTL core must guarantee that no two
operational cycles share identical cryptographic conditions, the cipher-state
is defined as a tuple:

\begin{equation}
    \Xi(t) = \big( N(t),\; K_{\text{auth}},\; S_{\text{cond}}(t) \big),
\end{equation}

where $N(t)$ is the ephemeral nonce, $K_{\text{auth}}$ is the hardwired
verification constant, and $S_{\text{cond}}(t)$ is the conditioned strain
register. The evolution rule is strictly monotonic:

\begin{equation}
    \Xi(t + \Delta t_{\text{femto}}) \neq \Xi(t),
\end{equation}

ensuring that the cryptographic enclosure cannot enter a repeated or
self-similar state. This property is essential for preventing replay-based
injection attacks across distributed simulation nodes.

\section{Authenticated Key-Gate Verification}
\label{sec:key-gate}

The RTL core enforces a single-step authenticated key-gate check at each
positive clock edge:

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{V}(t) =
    \begin{cases}
        1, & \text{if } K_{\text{auth}} = K_{\text{verify}}, \\
        0, & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}

If $\mathcal{V}(t) = 0$, the system enters immediate containment mode as
described in Section~\ref{sec:fault-containment}. Because the verification key
is embedded directly into the synthesized hardware fabric, the check executes
with zero additional latency, preserving the femtosecond timing guarantees of
the QLE control loop.

\section{Bias-Line Monotonicity Guarantee}
\label{sec:bias-monotonicity}

To prevent destructive resonance modes within the Josephson rectification
matrix, the RTL core must guarantee monotonic bias-line behavior across
successive femtosecond cycles. Let $V_{\text{bias}}(t)$ denote the output bias
value at time $t$. The monotonicity constraint is defined as:

\begin{equation}
    \big| V_{\text{bias}}(t + \Delta t_{\text{femto}}) -
           V_{\text{bias}}(t) \big|
    < \Lambda_{\text{max}},
\end{equation}

where $\Lambda_{\text{max}}$ is the maximum allowable bias deviation before
grid instability occurs. Because the OR/XOR mapping rules operate strictly on
the lower half-word of the conditioned strain register, the deviation bound is
maintained automatically without requiring additional arithmetic smoothing
filters.

\section{RTL Enclosure Determinism}
\label{sec:rtl-determinism}

Although the QLE v1.0 operates under high-frequency strain-field perturbations,
the cryptographic enclosure must remain fully deterministic. For any pair of
inputs $(S_{\text{raw}}, N)$, the RTL core produces a unique and reproducible
output:

\begin{equation}
    V_{\text{bias}} = f_{\text{RTL}}(S_{\text{raw}}, N),
\end{equation}

where $f_{\text{RTL}}$ is the bitwise mapping function defined in
Section~\ref{sec:bias-mapping}. This determinism ensures that distributed
simulation nodes can maintain synchronized harvesting-grid behavior even when
operating under independent cryptographic enclaves.

\section{Isolation of Cryptographic and Physical Domains}
\label{sec:domain-isolation}

The RTL enclosure is designed to prevent any direct coupling between the
cryptographic domain and the physical strain-sensing domain. The XOR-shard
conditioning layer enforces a strict separation:

\begin{equation}
    S_{\text{cond}} \not\rightarrow S_{\text{raw}},
\end{equation}

meaning that the conditioned strain register cannot be used to infer the raw
sensor values. Likewise, the bias output:

\begin{equation}
    V_{\text{bias}} \not\rightarrow N(t),
\end{equation}

ensures that the ephemeral nonce cannot be reconstructed from the harvesting
grid behavior. This bidirectional isolation is fundamental to the security
model of the QLE v1.0 cryptographic enclosure.